    "definition": "A rhetorical scaffold that repeats the same opening word or phrase across successive clauses to build clarity, rhythm, and force, typically in a memorable triad with a closing payoff line.",
    "help": "Pick a short anchor phrase the audience can predict (e.g., “We will”, “You can”, “Our data show”). Write three simple beats (one per line) that continue the anchor in parallel grammar. Escalate meaning across beats, then add a payoff sentence that concludes or decides. Use for policies, onboarding, research summaries, team principles, and public remarks. Keep the opener identical in wording and tense; stop at three unless more truly earns its keep.",
