    "definition": "Indigenous Storywork (Coast Salish — Sto:lo) is a relational, story-led dialogue practice that centers trust, reciprocity, dignity, and careful listening. Sessions are participant-led, paced by relationship and protocol rather than a rigid script. Closely related practices such as Yarning (Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander) and Talanoa (Pacific) share this ethos: relationship is method; reciprocity and ongoing consent are non-negotiable.",
    "help": "Use this template to plan, run, and return a story-led dialogue. It scaffolds relationship-first preparation, culturally appropriate openings, participant-led prompts, reciprocity and give-back, ongoing consent, member checking, and community-governed data practices.",
